  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-6685",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =|(-x)|+2 \\\\  & =|x|+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ the function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(|x|+2) \\\\  & =-|x|-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x),$ the function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
